// The release chain, walked end to end. AA4 and AF2 said it never had been.
//
// Not a slice: running a release is using the product, not delivering it. But it produced
// two defects and a product judgement, and findings need somewhere to live — three source
// comments and a commit message cite AS1 and AS2, and until this record existed every one of
// those was a citation into nothing, which is W16 exactly.

walkthrough "WALK.260822.02" {
    slug "cutting-a-release-for-the-first-time"
    title "What happens when bind, cut, verify and promote run in sequence"
    frame "FRAME.260819.01"
    walked-at "2026-08-22T00:00:00Z"
    walked-by "agent:principal-engineer"

    asks "thirty slices are delivered and seven symptoms are still `present`. The frame shrinks \
        by a RELEASE and no release has ever been cut. What is between here and one?"

    path "bind the closed iterations nothing binds"
    path "publish, commit, cut — and find out what order the tool actually requires"
    path "verify, promote, and ask whether a symptom can now be resolved"
    path "read the published set as a stranger would"
    path "withdraw the cut, once the reading was in"

    step "1" did="asked which closed iterations no release binds" \
        found="grep, not the tool. Seven of twenty-seven, all from today. The traversal exists in \
            the Corpus and there is no way to ask it — logged, and cut as TS.260821.11"
    step "2" did="bound all seven to 0.8.0" \
        found="each bind re-proposed the bump from the configured rules. Twenty-seven bound"
    step "3" did="ran cut-release" \
        found="REFUSED — the published set had uncommitted changes, so the commit about to be \
            indexed would not contain it. The tool taught the order: publish, commit, THEN cut"
    step "4" did="committed and ran cut-release again" \
        found="REFUSED — the-maintainer-confirms-the-version. The record proposes; choosing is \
            the maintainer's. Two refusals, two different rules, both correct"
    step "5" did="cut with --confirm" \
        found="0.8.0 cut at 8ddfd00. The index points at a commit and carries a seal"
    step "6" did="ran check" \
        found="six refusals. Five were promoted-truth-is-derived telling us the next command, \
            which is the chain working. The sixth was AS1"
    step "7" did="ran promote" \
        found="five capabilities went sought → active, with shipped counts derived from what bound. \
            The two PRODUCT capabilities did not, because no bound slice realizes them"
    step "8" did="ran verify-published" \
        found="verified, five documents unchanged. True, and it means less than it sounds — see AS3"
    step "10" did="withdrew the cut rather than adding a flag to republish over it" \
        found="the seal is only checked at state=released, so withdrawal is clean — but promotion \
            had no inverse (AS6), and `withdrawn` needed declaring twice (AS7). The prior \
            artifacts are archived at praxis/.usage/baseline/ as the thing the replacement has to beat"
    step "9" did="opened docs/releases/0.8.0 and read it as somebody who had read nothing else" \
        found="it is correct and not worth reading. That is AS4, and it is the finding of this walk"

    finding "AS1" \
        text="`praxis cut-release` recomposes the release node whole and dropped `proposed-bump`, \
            because the Corpus never held the field. Every cut would have dropped it" \
        note="what the record PROPOSED and what the maintainer CONFIRMED are two facts, and a cut \
            keeping only the second cannot be asked whether they agreed. Found by praxis check \
            immediately after the cut, which is the record catching the tool that writes it"

    finding "AS2" \
        text="`when-state` was declared on schema fields and never implemented. `proposed-bump` \
            said `each=1` unconditionally, so a cut release was required to carry a field whose \
            purpose is discharged at the cut" \
        obliges="scope proposed-bump to when-state=planned, and make when-state enforced" \
        note="a property nothing reads looks exactly like one that is honoured — the same shape \
            a-rule-has-a-witness exists to catch, one level down. `when-kind` beside it was real, \
            which is what made the omission invisible"

    finding "AS3" \
        text="verify-published compares the tree against the commit its release names, and passed \
            — while how-to-use-a-capability.md said `this release promoted no capability's truth` \
            and five capabilities had just been promoted" \
        obliges="AO2 — the published set depicts the record AT PUBLISH TIME, and the version stamp \
            is not the guarantee its wording implies" \
        note="the guarantee is `nobody hand-edited this`, which is real and is not `this is still \
            true`. Publishing before promoting is the ordinary order and it leaves the guide false, \
            so the chain has a step it does not state"

    finding "AS4" state="open" size="frame-sized" \
        text="the published set is correct and not worth reading. release-notes.md is a hundred \
            and seventy lines of iteration ids with one bump category repeated on every row; the \
            guide says nothing shipped while twelve `usage` lines sit unused in the capability \
            records" \
        obliges="TS.260821.12" \
        note="the record is MORE human-readable than its own projection. Every slice declares \
            title, trigger, outcome and useful-alone — sentences written by hand, for people — and \
            the Slice the corpus builds holds none of them. The publisher was never short of \
            material; it reads the accounting half. Being the right MECHANISM stood in for being \
            worth reading, for four slices and a release"

    finding "AS5" \
        text="no symptom was resolved. All seven have attacking slices bound to 0.8.0, and \
            resolution was not attempted because the walk stopped to fix what it found" \
        obliges="resolve the symptoms 0.8.0 can demonstrably attack, or record why each cannot" \
        note="the point of cutting was to make progress computable. It now is, and the computation \
            has not been run — which is a smaller gap than `no release exists` and is still a gap"

    finding "AS6" state="open" size="frame-sized" \
        text="withdrawing a cut release has no inverse for promotion. `praxis promote` refuses on a \
            release that is not cut, so the entries it already wrote into five capability records \
            stay — derived from a release that no longer stands, and unwindable only by hand" \
        obliges="promoted truth is DERIVED, so it must be recomputable downward as well as upward — \
            a recompute that reads every cut release and rewrites what each capability says it is" \
        note="the rule is named promoted-truth-is-derived and it caught the inconsistency \
            immediately, which is the record working. What it cannot do is fix it: every command in \
            this chain moves forward, and the one operation that moves back was performed with a \
            text editor"

    finding "AS7" state="open" size="observation" \
        text="`withdrawn` had to be added in TWO places on the release entity — `states=` on the \
            kind and `one-of=` on its `state` field — and the first edit alone left the refusal \
            unchanged, which read as the schema not having been picked up" \
        note="two declarations of one vocabulary, and nothing says they must agree. A kind whose \
            `states=` and whose `state` field's `one-of=` differ is a record that refuses \
            something it also permits"

    finding "AS9" state="open" size="observation" \
        text="`audit_excludes` in scripts/bump-version.sh was defined and never called, so the \
            exclude list in .version-bump.json read to nothing. Adding an entry to it changed \
            no behaviour, which looked like the entry being wrong" \
        note="third instance today of a declaration nothing reads — `when-state` on schema fields \
            (AS2), `needed-by` on read models, and now an audit exclude list. Each looks honoured \
            from the file that declares it, and only fails when somebody adds an entry expecting \
            it to work. A config key nothing reads is the enforcement version of an unwitnessed rule"

    proves "the release chain runs end to end, and refuses in the right places when it should not"
    does-not-prove "that a release is worth having. Four documents were produced and the walk's \
        strongest finding is that nobody would read them twice"

    trail {
        entry at="2026-08-22T00:00:00Z" by="agent:principal-engineer" action="created" \
            note="walked because AA4 and AF2 both said the chain never had been. Two defects and a \
                product judgement, in nine steps"
    }
}
